17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 22"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",19," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"Paging & the MMU"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"How a virtual address becomes a physical one, page tables, the walk, and the cache that makes it fast"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"In the last chapter we established the promise the OS makes to every process: a huge, private, contiguous address space that starts at zero and belongs to you alone. We also met the hardware that keeps that promise honest, the memory management unit, or MMU, sitting between the CPU and physical RAM, translating every address your program touches. This chapter is about the mechanism that makes it all work, and it is the mechanism that essentially every modern general-purpose OS uses: paging."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"The core move is almost embarrassingly simple. Chop the virtual address space into fixed-size blocks called pages, chop physical memory into equal-size blocks called frames, and keep a table that says which page lives in which frame. Any page can go in any frame; the pieces do not have to be contiguous or in order. That one idea dissolves the fragmentation problems that plagued older schemes and gives the OS fine-grained control over protection and sharing."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"We will build it up carefully and keep the arithmetic honest, because paging is a place where hand-waving hides real understanding. You will see exactly how the MMU splits an address into a page number and an offset, what each bit of a page-table entry is for, why a naive page table for a 64-bit machine would be preposterously large, how multi-level tables fix that, and how a small cache called the TLB stops all of this from crippling performance. Throughout, we flag why this shows up in your profiler and your production incidents, because it does, constantly."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-the-idea-of-paging",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-the-idea-of-paging","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The idea of paging"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-pages-frames-page-table",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-pages-frames-page-table","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":["$L21","$L22"]}]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27","$L28","$L29"]}]]}],"$L2a"]}],"$L2b"]}]
